Tier 4 -- policy against a fault-injecting mock adapter (workstation).

The fake-API tier. Every rung of the ladder is driven against tests/mock-
adapter.subr, which can return, per call: success, failure, timeout, garbage,
and empty output with a success status -- the crashed-verifier lesson encoded
as a test class. Every rung x every outcome has an expected disposition,
asserted as a truth table. See TESTING.md §5.

At M1 the ladder is fenced to its lineage and staleness rungs; quorum,
fencing, promotion proper and --force's rung-by-rung semantics arrive with M2
and M3, and their rows belong in this directory beside the ones that are here.

  t_mock_selftest.sh      the injector's own self-test: each scripted outcome
                          does what it says. An injector whose 'fail' silently
                          succeeded would turn every other row in this tier
                          green while proving nothing.
  t_ladder_lineage.sh     the M1 truth table: fresh / stale / skewed / absent
                          crossed with empty-with-success, garbage, failure,
                          usage error and no answer at all. Nothing but a
                          readable, fresh replica reaches "proceed".
  t_conformance_mock.sh   tests/conformance/vectors.tsv against the mock --
                          the same file tier 5 runs against real CBSD and
                          tier 6 against the pseudo-cluster adapter.
  t_adapter_parse.sh      the real adapter's parsers fed injected CBSD
                          listings. lib/adapter.subr cannot run here, but the
                          part of it that reads CBSD's output can, and that is
                          the part that breaks when CBSD changes a format.

Files named t_*.sh are tests; anything else here is ignored.

Since M3 this tier also holds t_sim_oracle_selftest.sh: tier 7's invariants
and cheap oracle fed hand-built broken states, with every invariant required
to be seen firing (TESTING.md §7, tests/cluster/sim/DESIGN.md §7). It is here
rather than in tests/cluster/sim/ so that 'sh tools/lint.sh' runs it on the
workstation on every pass -- an oracle checked once per reaper session is an
oracle nobody checks (D-56).
